# The cap ratchet's baseline. Rule 4.1, `plan/05:352-354`:
#
#   "the architecture test FAILS ON A CAP INCREASE IN THE DIFF, not just on a
#    violation. Vellum's caps only ever went down."
#
# That test did not exist. The default cap went 400 -> 800 with a green suite,
# because nothing was checking -- the rule was a wish (`plan/05` §0) for the
# whole of Milestone 1 and its tail. This file is the mechanism.
#
# HOW IT WORKS. `the_cap_ratchet_only_turns_down` in tests/ratchet.rs reads this
# file and compares it with the live constants in tests/file_rules.rs. A cap
# that is LOWER than its line here passes and is a ratchet turn. A cap that is
# HIGHER fails, and the only way to make it pass is to edit this file in the same
# commit -- which puts the increase in the diff, in a file that exists for no
# other purpose, where a reviewer cannot miss it.
#
# That is the honest version of "fails on a cap increase in the diff": a test
# cannot read a diff, but it can make an increase impossible to make quietly.
#
# WHEN YOU DO RAISE ONE. Say why here, in the commit, with a measurement. The
# 400 -> 800 change is the model: it was the author's call, it cited VellumFE's
# own caps (3600/2100/1700/1100/800/700) as evidence that 400 was invented, and
# it counted the cost -- five splits in one milestone.
#
# FORMAT: `<name> <value>`, one per line. `#` comments and blank lines ignored.

# The cap every file gets unless CAP_EXCEPTIONS names it.
#
# Was 400, raised to 800 by the author on 2026-09-18 after 400 forced five
# splits in one milestone. 400 was invented with no Cena code to calibrate
# against; 800 is below all but one of VellumFE's shipped caps.
DEFAULT_MAX_LINES 800

# How many named exceptions may exist at all.
#
# ONE: crates/cena-model/data/crit_tables.tsv, at 2400. It is the only file in
# the workspace for which "move code down" has no meaning -- it is DATA, 2,394
# table entries with no functions to move anywhere.
#
# A table that GROWS is a cap that is failing: every entry is a file that was
# allowed to keep growing instead of being split. This file's own 650-line
# exception was removed when the default rose to 800, which is the direction the
# count should travel.
#
# Recorded because I guessed 0 here and the test caught it -- which is the guard
# working on its author before anyone else.
MAX_CAP_EXCEPTIONS 1

# SPLIT PARENTS -- Rule 4.4's other half. `plan/05:385-388` cites Vellum's
# `split_parents_stay_facades` BY NAME, and Cena implemented only the
# `lib.rs`/`mod.rs` half: nine files with child modules -- state.rs, actor.rs,
# supervisor.rs, parser.rs, frame.rs and four more -- were governed by the 800
# default and by nothing else.
#
# Vellum's version is not a no-behavior rule. A split parent legitimately holds
# type definitions and dispatchers; `GameState::apply` belongs in `state.rs`.
# It is a PER-FILE CAP that only ratchets down, and its comment is the whole
# intent: "if one trips, move code down into a submodule instead of raising the
# cap."
#
# These are the measured sizes at 2026-09-19 rounded up to the next 50, which is
# how Vellum set its own ("the current size rounded up a little"). Headroom is
# deliberate and small: enough that an ordinary edit does not trip it, little
# enough that growth is a decision.
crates/cena/src/probe.rs 300
crates/cena-model/src/crit.rs 250
crates/cena-model/src/state.rs 500
crates/cena-protocol/src/frame.rs 450
crates/cena-protocol/src/parser.rs 450
crates/cena-protocol/src/tags.rs 350
crates/cena-protocol/src/text.rs 400
crates/cena-session/src/actor.rs 600
crates/cena-session/src/supervisor.rs 600
